// Generated by scripts/dev/ai-guides.js from docs/ai/*.md. Do not edit: change the guide, then run the script.
// The master guides for AI agents, as the server serves them (get_guide, .tribe/ai/ in a project folder).
(function (root, api) { if (typeof module === 'object' && module.exports) module.exports = api; else root.TribeAIGuides = api; })(typeof self !== 'undefined' ? self : this, {"README":"# TribeCode for AI agents: start here\n\nTribeCode is where a small group builds a project together: chat, tasks, docs, code, and three design tools (a website designer, App Creator for phone apps, and Motion Studio). You were invited by one of its people and work with their access, never more. This page tells you where to look; each tool has its own master guide.\n\n## The guides\n\n| Guide | Read it when you are asked to | Get it with |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [WEBSITE.md](WEBSITE.md) | build, restyle, rewrite or fix the project's website | `get_guide(\"website\")` |\n| [APP.md](APP.md) | design or change the project's phone app | `get_guide(\"app\")` |\n| [MOTION.md](MOTION.md) | make or change a motion graphic | `get_guide(\"motion\")` |\n\nEach guide stands on its own: the shape of the data, the tools in order, every step with an example, how to make the result good, a checklist, and what never to do. Over MCP the same guides are resources (`tribecode://guide/website` …) and prompts (`design_website`, `design_app`, `design_motion`).\n\n## Always first: the project brief\n\n`project_brief` returns what this project is: its name, the vision, what kind of thing it is, the audience and tone, the brand kit (colours, fonts, voice), what already exists (pages, screens, motion graphics), facts and decisions from the project's memory, open tasks and comments on the designs, and who approves what. It only holds what the person who invited you can see. Design from it. A fact that is not in it is a question for the person, never something to make up.\n\n## How every design tool works\n\n1. **Read** (`project_brief`, then `site_read` / `app_read` / `motion_read`).\n2. **Propose** steps (`site_propose` / `app_propose`), first with `dry_run: true`. A step that cannot be used is skipped and named, with a corrected example.\n3. **Check** (`site_check` / `app_check`) and fix what it lists.\n4. **A person approves.** They see before and after; one Undo takes it back. You cannot approve your own proposal.\n\n## What is always a person's to do\n\nPublishing, exporting, deleting pages or screens, uploading pictures, payments and accounts, and anything in a project you were not invited to.\n\n## In a project folder\n\nA project's designs are also real files in its folder (`site/`, `app-web/`). TribeCode writes an `AGENTS.md` there (and a `CLAUDE.md` that points to it) with what the project is and where things are, and copies of these guides in `.tribe/ai/` with the current brief as `.tribe/ai/PROJECT.md`. A file a person wrote themselves is never replaced.\n\n## Keeping these guides true\n\nThe lists in the guides (sections, layouts, parts, looks, fonts, kits, templates, steps) are written from the app's own data by `node scripts/dev/ai-guides.js`; `--check` runs in `npm test`. The design rules and the steps' examples live in `app/ui/aiguide-core.js`, which the MCP tools, \"Make it beautiful\", the copy-and-paste bridge and Flint's local model all read, so every path gives an AI the same advice.\n","WEBSITE":"# Building a website in TribeCode: the guide for AI agents\n\nYou are an AI working on a project's website inside TribeCode. This file is everything you need: what the website is made of, which tools to call in which order, every step you may propose, how to make the result good, and what you must never do. Follow it even if you have no other context.\n\n## 1. What you are working on\n\nA TribeCode website is **one design** per project: a JSON document of pages, each page a list of **sections** (also called blocks). A section has a `type` (hero, features, list …), a `variant` (its layout), and `props` (its words). One **theme** styles the whole site. People edit the same design in the website designer: a list of blocks on the left, the page in the middle, and Edit / Ideas / Style / Check tabs on the right. Your proposal arrives there as a suggested change with a before and an after.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Bean and Gone\",\n  \"theme\": { \"preset\": \"paper\", \"mode\": \"light\", \"colors\": { \"bg\": \"#F1EADB\", \"ink\": \"#2B241C\", \"accent\": \"#B4532C\" },\n             \"fonts\": { \"display\": \"Instrument Serif\", \"body\": \"System sans\" }, \"radius\": 10, \"space\": \"normal\" },\n  \"pages\": [\n    { \"id\": \"p1\", \"slug\": \"index\", \"title\": \"Home\", \"blocks\": [\n      { \"id\": \"b1\", \"type\": \"hero\", \"variant\": \"split\",\n        \"props\": { \"eyebrow\": \"Mill Street\", \"title\": \"Coffee roasted next door\", \"text\": \"Open every day from 7.\",\n                   \"img\": \"\", \"alt\": \"\", \"buttons\": [ { \"label\": \"See the menu\", \"link\": { \"kind\": \"section\", \"to\": \"b2\" } } ] } },\n      { \"id\": \"b2\", \"type\": \"list\", \"variant\": \"menu\", \"style\": { \"bg\": \"card\" },\n        \"props\": { \"title\": \"Menu\", \"items\": [ { \"title\": \"Flat white\", \"meta\": \"$4\", \"text\": \"\" } ] } }\n    ] }\n  ],\n  \"seo\": { \"title\": \"\", \"description\": \"\" }\n}\n```\n\nYou never send this document. You send **steps** (ops), TribeCode applies them to the design, checks each one, and a person approves the result. The design is also written as real files into the project folder (`site/index.html`, `site/site.json` …) a moment after every save; those files are output, not the place to edit.\n\n## 2. The tools, in order\n\n| # | Call | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | `project_brief` | What this project is: vision, audience, tone, brand kit, what exists, facts, open tasks and comments, who approves. Read it before you write a word. |\n| 2 | `site_catalog` | Every section type with its layouts and the words it holds, the looks, the font pairs, the illustrations, and every step with an example. (The same lists are below.) |\n| 3 | `site_read` | The design with ids. `detail: \"summary\"` gives the structure only; `page` gives one page in full. You need the ids for your steps. |\n| 4 | `site_view` | The page as a person reads it, in plain text. `device: \"phone\"` shows what a phone hides and how long things get. |\n| 5 | `site_propose` with `dry_run: true` | Tries your steps on a copy and tells you what each did, what was skipped and why (with a corrected example), and what Check would say. Nothing is sent. |\n| 6 | `site_propose` | Sends the proposal. A person sees before and after and approves; it is one Undo. |\n| 7 | `site_check` with `proposed: true` | \"Check my site\" on your proposal: what to fix (links, forms, contrast, empty parts, example words) and tips. Fix what you can with more steps. |\n| 8 | `site_comments` | What people asked to change, by section. Answer them in your steps. |\n\nRules of the tools:\n\n- **No website yet?** Call `site_propose` with `start: { \"template\": \"<id>\" }` or `start: { \"blank\": true }` plus your steps. `site_catalog` lists the templates. Starting is a proposal too.\n- **Ids of new things.** A section you add gets its id when the proposal is sent, and the answer lists every section with its id. Put everything a new section needs into its `add` step's `props`; to change it further, send the proposal, read the ids from the answer, and send more steps (they join the same proposal). Ids in a dry run's answer are examples only.\n- **Your waiting proposal** is not the saved site yet. `site_read`, `site_view` and `site_check` take `proposed: true` to look at the site as it will be if your proposal is approved. Check that, not the old site.\n- **More than 60 steps** do not fit one call. Call `site_propose` again: while your proposal is still waiting, new steps are added on top of it and the person still sees one request. `fresh: true` starts over instead.\n- **Skipped steps** are named with the reason and an example. Fix them and send only the fixed ones.\n- **Your access** is the access of the person who invited you, never more, and they can switch the website off for you. A refusal is an answer: do not look for another way in.\n\n## 3. Sections and their layouts\n\n| type | What it is | Layouts (variant) | Words it holds (props) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `hero` | Hero | center, split, minimal, accent, cover | eyebrow, title, text, img, alt, shape, buttons[label] |\n| `features` | Features | grid, list, stats, alternating, icons, ticks | title, items[title, text] |\n| `gallery` | Gallery | grid, masonry, strip | title, items[img, alt, caption] |\n| `pricing` | Pricing | cards, table | title, note, plans[name, price, per, features, button] |\n| `testimonials` | Testimonials | cards, quotes, spotlight | title, items[quote, name, role] |\n| `faq` | FAQ | list, columns | title, items[q, a] |\n| `team` | Team | grid, list | title, items[name, role, img, alt] |\n| `links` | Links (link in bio) | stack, cards, pills | name, bio, img, alt, shape, items[label, note], socials[words] |\n| `list` | List (menu, dates, episodes) | rows, cards, menu | title, items[title, meta, text] |\n| `contact` | Contact form | form, simple | title, text, email, action, button |\n| `embed` | Video / embed | wide, inline | title, url, caption |\n| `text` | Text | plain, two, highlight | title, text |\n| `footer` | Footer | simple, columns, minimal | text, links[words] |\n| `newsletter` | Newsletter | inline, card | title, text, action, button |\n| `cta` | Call to action | simple, split, accent | title, text, buttons[label] |\n| `stats` | Stats | grid, bar, big | title, items[value, label] |\n| `logos` | Logo cloud | row, grid | title, items[name, img, alt] |\n| `banner` | Banner | bar, ribbon | text, button{label} |\n| `divider` | Divider | line, space, dots |  |\n| `image` | Image | wide, side, framed | title, text, img, alt, shape |\n| `steps` | Steps (how it works) | row, stack | title, items[title, text] |\n| `info` | Visit us (address, hours, phone) | columns, card | title, items[label, text], button{label} |\n| `go` | Send visitors on (redirect) | card, plain | title, text, to, wait, button |\n| `timeline` | Timeline (story, experience) | line, cards | title, items[when, title, text] |\n\n`props` in an `add` step may only use the keys in the last column, with the same kind of value (words stay words, lists stay lists). Pictures (`img`) cannot be set by you.\n\n## 4. Looks, fonts, illustrations\n\nA look sets colours, fonts and corners at once (`theme`). `palette` takes only its colours. Every look has a light and a dark version (`mode`).\n\n| preset | Name | Mode | Accent | Fonts | Corners |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `paper` | Paper | light | #B4532C | Instrument Serif + System sans | 10 |\n| `swiss` | Swiss | light | #E2361B | Space Grotesk + Inter | 0 |\n| `editorial` | Editorial | light | #8C3B22 | Fraunces + Inter | 4 |\n| `terminal` | Terminal | dark | #5BE37D | JetBrains Mono + JetBrains Mono | 2 |\n| `playful` | Playful | light | #FF5A4E | Bricolage Grotesque + Inter | 18 |\n| `minimal` | Minimal | light | #2C5BE0 | Inter + Inter | 8 |\n| `bold` | Bold | dark | #F7D13D | Bricolage Grotesque + Inter | 0 |\n| `soft` | Soft | light | #7A5CCB | Fraunces + Inter | 16 |\n| `night` | Dark Minimal | dark | #22C55E | Space Grotesk + Inter | 8 |\n| `ivory` | Ivory | light | #4F46E5 | Space Grotesk + Inter | 10 |\n| `clean` | Clean | light | #1C7ED6 | Inter + Inter | 8 |\n| `sand` | Sand | light | #B45309 | Bricolage Grotesque + Inter | 10 |\n| `amber` | Amber | dark | #E8A838 | Bricolage Grotesque + Inter | 6 |\n| `ocean` | Ocean | dark | #60A5FA | Space Grotesk + Inter | 8 |\n| `rose` | Rosé | dark | #F472B6 | Fraunces + Inter | 12 |\n| `haze` | Purple Haze | dark | #A78BFA | Space Grotesk + Inter | 10 |\n| `slate` | Slate | dark | #06B6D4 | Space Grotesk + Inter | 6 |\n| `forest` | Forest | dark | #10B981 | Inter + Inter | 8 |\n\nFont pairs (`fonts`): \n`classic` (Fraunces + Inter), `editorial` (Instrument Serif + Inter), `modern` (Space Grotesk + Inter), `loud` (Bricolage Grotesque + Inter), `plain` (Inter), `code` (JetBrains Mono), `pixel` (Silkscreen + Inter), `book` (Fraunces + System serif), `elegant` (DM Serif Display + Inter), `friendly` (Outfit + Inter), `playfair` (Playfair Display + Lato), `lora` (Lora + Karla), `merriweather` (Merriweather + Work Sans), `baskerville` (Libre Baskerville + Raleway), `cormorant` (Cormorant Garamond + Montserrat), `garamond` (EB Garamond + Figtree), `crimson` (Crimson Pro + IBM Plex Sans), `spectral` (Spectral + Manrope), `sourceserif` (Source Serif 4 + Public Sans), `bitter` (Bitter + Chivo), `zilla` (Zilla Slab + Hanken Grotesk), `arvo` (Arvo + Lato), `abril` (Abril Fatface + Poppins), `alfaslab` (Alfa Slab One + Rubik), `bebas` (Bebas Neue + DM Sans), `anton` (Anton + Barlow), `oswald` (Oswald + Source Sans 3), `archivo` (Archivo Black + Archivo), `jakarta` (Plus Jakarta Sans), `manrope` (Manrope + Inter), `plex` (IBM Plex Sans + IBM Plex Serif), `plexmono` (IBM Plex Mono + IBM Plex Sans), `spacemono` (Space Mono + IBM Plex Sans), `firacode` (Fira Code + Fira Sans), `rubik` (Rubik + Karla), `nunito` (Nunito), `quicksand` (Quicksand + Nunito), `poppins` (Poppins + Inter), `montserrat` (Montserrat + Merriweather), `raleway` (Raleway + Lora), `josefin` (Josefin Sans + Lato), `syne` (Syne + Inter), `sora` (Sora + DM Sans), `lexend` (Lexend), `unbounded` (Unbounded + Figtree), `righteous` (Righteous + Nunito), `pacifico` (Pacifico + Quicksand), `caveat` (Caveat + Karla), `lobster` (Lobster + Lato), `arcade` (Press Start 2P + Space Mono), `terminal` (VT323 + IBM Plex Mono), `epilogue` (Epilogue + Inter), `youngserif` (Young Serif + Instrument Sans), `gloock` (Gloock + Hanken Grotesk), `bodoni` (Bodoni Moda + Figtree), `cinzel` (Cinzel + Lato), `comfortaa` (Comfortaa + Nunito), `fredoka` (Fredoka + Nunito), `atkinson` (Atkinson Hyperlegible), `literata` (Literata), `newsreader` (Newsreader + Inter), `schibsted` (Schibsted Grotesk + Source Serif 4), `shrikhand` (Shrikhand + Rubik), `monoton` (Monoton + Barlow), `kalam` (Kalam + Karla), `redhat` (Red Hat Display + Red Hat Text), `baloo` (Baloo 2 + Nunito), `librefranklin` (Libre Franklin + Libre Baskerville)\n\nIllustrations for empty picture slots (`illustration`), drawn in the site's own colours: \n`hills` (hills and sun), `cafe` (coffee cup), `desk` (desk and laptop), `shop` (shop front), `phone` (phone app), `people` (two people), `rocket` (rocket), `stage` (stage and mic)\n\n## 5. The steps (ops)\n\nEach step is `{ \"op\": …, … }`. `block` is always a section id from `site_read`. `path` names words inside a section: `title`, `text`, `items.0.title`, `buttons.1.label`, `plans.0.price`.\n\n- `add`: Add a section. props are words only and must use the keys that section has (site_catalog lists them).\n  `{\"op\":\"add\",\"type\":\"list\",\"variant\":\"menu\",\"after\":\"<block id>\",\"props\":{\"title\":\"Menu\",\"items\":[{\"title\":\"Flat white\",\"meta\":\"$4\",\"text\":\"Double shot, steamed milk\"}]}}`\n- `text`: Change words. path is a key of the section (title, text, items.0.title, buttons.0.label, plans.1.price).\n  `{\"op\":\"text\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"title\",\"value\":\"Coffee roasted on Mill Street\"}`\n- `variant`: Change a section's layout to another of its own variants.\n  `{\"op\":\"variant\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"variant\":\"split\"}`\n- `style`: A section's background (\"\" plain, card, accent, ink) and spacing (\"\" normal, s tight, l roomy).\n  `{\"op\":\"style\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"bg\":\"card\",\"pad\":\"l\"}`\n- `move`: Move a section: up, down, top, bottom or a place number from 0.\n  `{\"op\":\"move\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"to\":\"up\"}`\n- `remove`: Take a section out. Say why in the note.\n  `{\"op\":\"remove\",\"block\":\"<block id>\"}`\n- `link`: Point a button or entry somewhere. path is the button (buttons.0, button, items.2); kind is page, section, url (https only) or email.\n  `{\"op\":\"link\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"buttons.0\",\"kind\":\"section\",\"to\":\"<block id of the target>\"}`\n- `item`: Entries of a list: do is add, dup, del, up or down; path is the list (items, plans, buttons), i the entry.\n  `{\"op\":\"item\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"items\",\"i\":0,\"do\":\"add\"}`\n- `illustration`: Fill an empty picture slot with a built-in drawing in the site's colours. path \"\" for the section's own picture, items.1 for an entry's.\n  `{\"op\":\"illustration\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"\",\"name\":\"cafe\"}`\n- `alt`: Describe a picture for people who cannot see it.\n  `{\"op\":\"alt\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"\",\"value\":\"The counter at Bean and Gone with the espresso machine\"}`\n- `show`: Hide a section (or one part of it, with path) on some screen sizes: phone, tablet, desktop. hide \"\" shows it everywhere.\n  `{\"op\":\"show\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"hide\":\"phone\"}`\n- `estyle`: One element inside a section: sz s|l|xl, col accent|muted|ink, al left|center|right, b and i true|false.\n  `{\"op\":\"estyle\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"title\",\"set\":{\"sz\":\"l\",\"al\":\"center\"}}`\n- `entrance`: A quiet entrance as the section scrolls in: fade, rise or \"\" for none.\n  `{\"op\":\"entrance\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"value\":\"fade\"}`\n- `theme`: The whole look at once: colours, fonts and corners of a named look. The site keeps the light or dark version it had: follow with mode to switch.\n  `{\"op\":\"theme\",\"preset\":\"paper\"}`\n- `palette`: Only the colours of a named look; fonts and corners stay.\n  `{\"op\":\"palette\",\"preset\":\"sand\"}`\n- `accent`: The one main colour (buttons, links). It is adjusted until it reads on the background.\n  `{\"op\":\"accent\",\"color\":\"#B4532C\"}`\n- `fonts`: A font pair: one display face for headings, one for reading.\n  `{\"op\":\"fonts\",\"pair\":\"editorial\"}`\n- `mode`: The dark or the light version of the look.\n  `{\"op\":\"mode\",\"value\":\"dark\"}`\n- `space`: Spacing for the whole site: compact, normal or roomy.\n  `{\"op\":\"space\",\"value\":\"roomy\"}`\n- `radius`: Corner radius in pixels, 0 to 28.\n  `{\"op\":\"radius\",\"value\":6}`\n- `buttons`: Button shape for the whole site: solid, outline, pill or soft.\n  `{\"op\":\"buttons\",\"value\":\"pill\"}`\n- `page`: Add a page. Contact, About, Pricing and FAQ titles get fitting sections to start from.\n  `{\"op\":\"page\",\"title\":\"Menu\"}`\n- `rename`: Rename a page (by its id, slug or title).\n  `{\"op\":\"rename\",\"page\":\"About\",\"title\":\"Our story\"}`\n- `name`: The site's name.\n  `{\"op\":\"name\",\"value\":\"Bean and Gone\"}`\n- `seo`: The title and the sentence search results and link previews show (description up to about 155 characters).\n  `{\"op\":\"seo\",\"title\":\"Bean and Gone · Coffee on Mill Street\",\"description\":\"Small-batch coffee roasted on Mill Street. Open every day from 7.\"}`\n\nA whole section in one step:\n\n```json\n{ \"op\": \"add\", \"type\": \"info\", \"variant\": \"columns\", \"after\": \"b2\",\n  \"props\": { \"title\": \"Visit us\", \"items\": [\n    { \"label\": \"Address\", \"text\": \"12 Mill Street\" }, { \"label\": \"Hours\", \"text\": \"Every day, 7 to 4\" } ] } }\n```\n\n## 6. How to make it good\n\n### Start from the project\n\n- Read the project brief first. Decide in one sentence who visits this site and the one thing they should do there (book, buy, visit, sign up, write). Every section either helps that or goes.\n- Pick the kind of site from the brief, then a recipe below. Change it to fit: a recipe is a starting order, not a template.\n- At least one section must be something only this project has: its menu, its prices, its dates, its hours, its story, its numbers.\n\n### Choose a layout that fits the kind of site\n\n| Kind of site | Sections, in order (type · layout) | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A place people visit (café, shop, salon, studio) | hero · split → list · menu → info · columns → gallery · grid → testimonials · quotes → cta · accent → footer · columns | People want to know what you sell, when you are open and where you are. |\n| A product or an app | hero · split → logos · row → features · alternating → steps · row → pricing · cards → faq · list → cta · accent → footer · columns | Say what it does, show it working, answer the price question. |\n| A portfolio or a studio | hero · minimal → gallery · masonry → text · two → testimonials · spotlight → contact · simple → footer · minimal | The work is the page. Words stay short. |\n| A service (trades, coaching, freelance) | hero · center → features · ticks → steps · stack → testimonials · cards → pricing · table → contact · form → footer · simple | Trust first, then how it works, then a way to ask. |\n| An event, a band, a podcast | hero · cover → list · rows → embed · wide → newsletter · card → footer · simple | What, when, where, then a way to follow. |\n| A cause or a community | hero · accent → stats · big → text · highlight → team · grid → cta · split → footer · columns | Why it matters, proof, who is behind it, how to join. |\n| A person (résumé, about me) | hero · minimal → timeline · line → features · list → contact · simple → footer · minimal | Who, what they have done, how to reach them. |\n| One link for a bio | links · stack | One screen, nothing to scroll past. |\n\n### The craft\n\n**Layout**\n- Five to eight sections on the home page. More is a second page (op page).\n- No two neighbours with the same layout. Alternate: words beside a picture, then a list, then a band of colour.\n- Use the layout that fits the content: a menu or dates are a list, not feature cards; numbers are stats; a story is text or a timeline.\n- One hero per page. Other pages start with hero minimal.\n\n**Type**\n- One font pair for the whole site (op fonts). A serif display face for warm, crafted, food, editorial; a grotesk for products and tools; a loud face only for events and bold brands.\n- The brand kit's fonts win when the brief has them.\n- Hero title: 3 to 8 words that say what this is, in the project's own words. Hero text: one or two sentences. Section titles: 2 to 5 words.\n- A paragraph is at most three sentences. Longer becomes a list, steps or an FAQ.\n- Make at most one thing per section bigger or coloured (op estyle). Emphasis everywhere is emphasis nowhere.\n\n**Spacing**\n- Set the rhythm once (op space): roomy for few words and big pictures, compact for menus, prices and reference pages.\n- Keep section spacing the same down the page. At most one section is tighter or roomier (op style, pad).\n- Separate sections with background steps: plain, card, plain. One accent or ink band per page, usually the call to action. Never two loud bands in a row.\n\n**Colour**\n- One accent colour. Take it from the brand kit; with none, pick one that fits the trade and leave the rest to the look's neutrals.\n- Words need a contrast of 4.5 to 1 on their background, the accent 3 to 1. site_check reports both.\n- Choose the look (op theme) for the mood first, then the light or dark version (op mode), then set the accent. Dark looks suit evenings, music, games and tools; light looks suit food, shops, services and anything read in daylight.\n\n**Pictures**\n- You cannot upload pictures. Fill an empty picture slot with a built-in illustration (op illustration) or choose a layout that needs none (hero minimal, center or accent).\n- Give every picture a description (op alt) that says what is in it.\n- In your note, list the pictures the person should add (the shop front, the team, the product).\n\n**Words**\n- Write from the brief's facts: the real name, what it sells, where, when, for whom, in the brand kit's voice.\n- A fact you do not have (a price, an address, opening hours, a quote) is never made up. Leave the section out, or write plainly what belongs there and name it in your note as something the person fills in.\n- Buttons say what happens: \"See the menu\", \"Book a table\", \"Write to us\". Every button is linked (op link).\n- No example words left behind. site_check lists sections that still have them.\n\n**Phones**\n- On a phone everything stacks into one column, so length is the cost. Lists of more than six entries, more than three price plans or a long hero text make a long scroll.\n- Use pricing cards rather than a table when there are more than four rows. Avoid words longer than about 30 letters.\n- Hide what is only decoration on phones (op show, hide \"phone\"). Never hide the way to contact, buy or visit.\n- Read site_view with device \"phone\" before you finish.\n\n**Access**\n- Every picture has a description, every button says where it goes, every section has a title.\n- Set the search title and description (op seo).\n\n### What to avoid\n\n- The generic page: a centred hero, three feature cards, a call to action. If your plan is that, start again from the brief.\n- Placeholder words: lorem ipsum, \"Welcome to our website\", \"Your tagline here\", the example words a new section comes with.\n- Walls of text, and sections that repeat what the one above said.\n- More than one accent colour, or a different background on every section.\n- Invented testimonials, logos, numbers, prices, awards or team members.\n- Buttons that go nowhere, forms that send nowhere, an empty gallery.\n\n## 7. Before you say it is done\n\n- [ ] site_check returns no \"fix\" items you can fix (links, forms, descriptions, contrast, empty sections).\n- [ ] No section has example words; no fact is invented; what is missing is named in the note.\n- [ ] The first screen says what this is and has one clear button.\n- [ ] site_view on \"phone\": nothing important is hidden, no list is endless.\n- [ ] One accent colour, one font pair, an even spacing rhythm.\n- [ ] The search title and description are set.\n- [ ] Your note says in two sentences what you changed and why, then what the person should add.\n\nThen tell the person, in plain words: what you proposed, where to approve it (Suggested changes in the website designer, or their inbox), and what only they can add (pictures, facts you did not have).\n\n## 8. What you must never do\n\n- Publish, download or export the site. A person does that.\n- Edit the files in the project folder's site/ directory while TribeCode is connected: they are written from the design and your edit would be lost or held back. Propose steps instead.\n- Add scripts, trackers, embeds from addresses the person did not give, or your own CSS.\n- Work in a project you were not asked about, or copy anything from another project.\n- Invent facts about the project, or leave a made-up fact for the person to catch.\n- Approve your own proposal, or propose again and again to get past a \"no\".\n\n## 9. A worked example: a café site from nothing\n\n1. `project_brief` says: \"Bean and Gone, a small coffee roaster on Mill Street. Open every day 7 to 4.\" Brand accent `#B4532C`, voice \"warm, short sentences\". No address number, no prices.\n2. Kind of site: a place people visit. Recipe: hero split → list menu → info → gallery → testimonials → cta → footer. No prices and no quotes are known, so the menu lists drinks without prices and testimonials are left out.\n3. `site_propose` with `dry_run: true`:\n\n```json\n{ \"start\": { \"blank\": true }, \"note\": \"A first site for Bean and Gone from the vision: what it is, the menu, where and when.\",\n  \"ops\": [\n    { \"op\": \"theme\", \"preset\": \"paper\" }, { \"op\": \"accent\", \"color\": \"#B4532C\" }, { \"op\": \"name\", \"value\": \"Bean and Gone\" },\n    { \"op\": \"add\", \"type\": \"list\", \"variant\": \"menu\", \"props\": { \"title\": \"On the bar\", \"items\": [ { \"title\": \"Espresso\", \"meta\": \"\", \"text\": \"Our own roast\" } ] } },\n    { \"op\": \"add\", \"type\": \"info\", \"variant\": \"columns\", \"props\": { \"title\": \"Find us\", \"items\": [ { \"label\": \"Where\", \"text\": \"Mill Street\" }, { \"label\": \"Hours\", \"text\": \"Every day, 7 to 4\" } ] } },\n    { \"op\": \"seo\", \"title\": \"Bean and Gone · Coffee on Mill Street\", \"description\": \"A small coffee roaster on Mill Street. Open every day from 7 to 4.\" }\n  ] }\n```\n\n4. Send it (without `dry_run`). The answer lists every section with its id. A second call (it adds to the same proposal) rewrites the hero with `text` steps, sets its layout with `variant`, links its button to the menu with `link`, fills its picture with `illustration`, and removes the starter sections that do not fit.\n5. `site_check` with `proposed: true`: no \"fix\" left that you can fix. `site_view` with `proposed: true, device: \"phone\"`: the menu has six entries, nothing is hidden.\n6. Tell the person: approve it under Suggested changes; add a photo of the shop, the street number and prices.\n","APP":"# Designing a phone app in TribeCode: the guide for AI agents\n\nYou are an AI working on a project's phone app design inside TribeCode's App Creator. This file is everything you need: what the design is made of, which tools to call in which order, every step you may propose, how to make the result good, and what you must never do. Follow it even if you have no other context.\n\n## 1. What you are working on\n\nAn app is **one design** per project: a JSON document of **screens**, each a list of **parts** (blocks). A part has a `type` (header, list, button …), `props` (its words and example data) and `links` (where a tap goes). One **theme** styles every screen. People edit the same design in App Creator: screens on the left, the phone in the middle, the selection and Flint on the right, and they can play it as a prototype. It exports to a phone web app and an Expo project; a person does that.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Bean and Gone\", \"platform\": \"ios\", \"start\": \"home\",\n  \"theme\": { \"accent\": \"#B4532C\", \"mode\": \"light\", \"radius\": \"soft\", \"font\": \"friendly\", \"density\": \"regular\",\n             \"icons\": \"regular\", \"motion\": \"standard\", \"surface\": \"warm\", \"preset\": \"\" },\n  \"screens\": [\n    { \"id\": \"home\", \"name\": \"Home\", \"tab\": true, \"blocks\": [\n      { \"id\": \"hea-1\", \"type\": \"header\", \"props\": { \"eyebrow\": \"Mill Street\", \"title\": \"Good morning\", \"sub\": \"Order ahead, skip the queue\" }, \"links\": {} },\n      { \"id\": \"but-1\", \"type\": \"button\", \"props\": { \"label\": \"Start an order\", \"style\": \"primary\", \"icon\": \"coffee\" },\n        \"links\": { \"\": { \"to\": \"menu\", \"how\": \"push\" } } }\n    ] },\n    { \"id\": \"menu\", \"name\": \"Menu\", \"blocks\": [] }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nA link's key is the path of the thing that is tapped: `\"\"` is the part itself (a button), `items.2` is the third entry of a list or tab bar. `how` is the transition: `push` (a new screen slides in), `sheet` (slides up over the screen), `fade` (tabs), `morph` (the tapped thing grows into the next screen).\n\nYou never send this document. You send **steps** (ops); TribeCode checks each one, and a person approves the result. The app is also written as files into the project folder (`app-web/`, with `app-web/app-design.json`); those files are output, not the place to edit.\n\n## 2. The tools, in order\n\n| # | Call | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | `project_brief` | What this project is: vision, audience, tone, brand kit, what exists, facts, open tasks, who approves. Read it before you write a word. |\n| 2 | `app_catalog` | Every part with the words it holds, the kits, looks, fonts, icons and pictures, the templates, and every step with an example. (The same lists are below.) |\n| 3 | `app_read` | The design with ids. `detail: \"summary\"` gives the structure only; `screen` gives one screen in full. You need the ids for your steps. |\n| 4 | `app_view` | Each screen as a person reads it, in plain text, with where every tap goes and which screens nothing leads to. |\n| 5 | `app_propose` with `dry_run: true` | Tries your steps on a copy: what each did, what was skipped and why (with a corrected example), the new ids, and what the check would say. Nothing is sent. |\n| 6 | `app_propose` | Sends the proposal. A person sees the app before and after and approves; it is one Undo. |\n| 7 | `app_check` with `proposed: true` | What a careful designer would point out in your proposal: buttons that go nowhere, screens nothing opens, empty parts, colour that does not read. Fix what you can and propose again. |\n\nRules of the tools:\n\n- **No app yet?** Call `app_propose` with `start: { \"template\": \"<id>\" }` or `start: { \"blank\": true }` plus your steps. Starting is a proposal too.\n- **Ids of new things.** A screen or part you add gets its id when the proposal is sent, and the answer lists every screen and part with its id. To change something new, send the proposal, read the ids from the answer, and send more steps (they join the same proposal). Ids in a dry run's answer are examples only.\n- **Your waiting proposal** is not the saved design yet. `app_read`, `app_view` and `app_check` take `proposed: true` to look at the app as it will be if your proposal is approved. Check that, not the old design.\n- **More than 60 steps** do not fit one call. Call `app_propose` again: while your proposal is still waiting, new steps are added on top of it and the person still sees one request. `fresh: true` starts over instead.\n- **Skipped steps** are named with the reason and an example. Fix them and send only the fixed ones.\n- **Your access** is the access of the person who invited you, never more, and they can switch designs off for you. A refusal is an answer: do not look for another way in.\n\n## 3. Parts\n\n| Group | Parts (type: what it is) |\n| --- | --- |\n| Navigation | `nav` top bar, `tabs` tab bar, `segmented` segmented control |\n| Text | `header` big title, `text` paragraph, `quote` quote, `alert` notice, `label` section label |\n| Content | `list` list, `cards` card row, `card` feature card, `settings` settings rows, `checklist` checklist, `grid` tile grid, `accordion` questions, `kanban` board columns |\n| Input | `form` form, `search` search and chips, `button` button, `calendar` day and time picker, `toggles` switch rows, `slider` slider, `stepper` number stepper, `tags` tags, `keypad` number pad, `swatches` colour picker |\n| Social | `chat` chat, `feed` feed post, `profile` profile header, `rating` rating, `stories` story circles, `contact` contact card, `comments` comments, `notice` notification card, `poll` poll |\n| Flows | `onboarding` welcome pages, `signin` sign in, `empty` empty state, `paywall` paywall layout, `steps` steps, `otp` code boxes |\n| Data | `chart` chart, `stats` stat tiles, `timer` timer, `progress` progress bar, `weather` weather card, `countdown` countdown, `rings` activity rings, `table` table |\n| Media | `player` media player, `map` map, `image` picture, `carousel` picture carousel, `voice` voice note, `video` video player, `hero` big picture header |\n| Commerce | `product` product card, `receipt` receipt, `tracker` order tracker, `wallet` card picture, `coupon` coupon |\n\n`app_catalog` gives the words each part holds (its `props`). A part is added with example content; change it with `text` steps.\n\n## 4. Kits: ready-made screens and flows\n\nA kit adds one or more finished screens (`{ \"op\": \"kit\", \"kit\": \"settings\" }`). Use one before you build a screen by hand, then rewrite its words for this project.\n\n`settings`, `onboarding`, `paywall`, `profile`, `signin`, `chat`, `shop`, `charts`, `empty`, `player`, `search`, `notifications`, `checkout`, `detail`, `feed`, `calendar`, `timer`, `map`, `help`, `about`, `leaderboard`, `achievements`, `invite`, `permissions`, `order-tracking`, `product-page`, `reviews`, `verify-code`, `send-money`, `my-cards`, `weather`, `activity`, `countdown`, `poll`, `stories`, `voice-notes`, `lesson`, `board`, `coupon`, `interests`, `booking-details`, `help-center`, `offline`, `settings-notify`, `theme-picker`, `notifications2`, `event-page`, `goal-setup`, `team-contacts`, `faq`, `whats-new`\n\n## 5. Looks, fonts, pictures\n\nA look (`preset`) sets accent, mode, type, corners, spacing, surface, motion and icon weight at once. Single controls are changed with `theme`.\n\nLight: `ember`, `meadow`, `harbor`, `paper`, `bloom`, `citrus`, `lavender`, `slate`, `terracotta`, `lagoon`, `sunbeam`, `mono`, `coral`, `mint`, `cobalt`, `rosewater`, `orchard`, `glacier`, `studio`, `playground`, `zen`, `candy`, `sandstone`, `ledger`, `gallery`, `nursery`, `clinic`, `festival`, `picnic`, `library`, `pixel-day`, `fieldnotes`, `pop`, `system-ios`\n\nDark: `campfire`, `forest`, `midnight`, `ink`, `velvet`, `arcade`, `dusk`, `graphite`, `espresso`, `deepsea`, `noir`, `neon`, `ember-pro`, `cinema`, `stormy`, `retro`, `aurora`, `sport`, `bank`, `blueprint`, `lunar`, `system-dark`\n\nFonts (`theme`, `font`): \n`system` (System), `clean` (Clean (Inter)), `friendly` (Friendly (Outfit + Inter)), `bold` (Bold (Bricolage Grotesque + Inter)), `tech` (Tech (Space Grotesk + Inter)), `editorial` (Editorial (Fraunces + Inter)), `elegant` (Elegant (DM Serif Display + Inter)), `classic` (Classic (Instrument Serif + Inter)), `mono` (Mono (JetBrains Mono + Inter)), `pixel` (Pixel (Silkscreen + JetBrains Mono)), `playfair` (Playfair Display + Lato), `lora` (Lora + Karla), `merriweather` (Merriweather + Work Sans), `baskerville` (Libre Baskerville + Raleway), `cormorant` (Cormorant Garamond + Montserrat), `garamond` (EB Garamond + Figtree), `crimson` (Crimson Pro + IBM Plex Sans), `spectral` (Spectral + Manrope), `sourceserif` (Source Serif 4 + Public Sans), `bitter` (Bitter + Chivo), `zilla` (Zilla Slab + Hanken Grotesk), `arvo` (Arvo + Lato), `abril` (Abril Fatface + Poppins), `alfaslab` (Alfa Slab One + Rubik), `bebas` (Bebas Neue + DM Sans), `anton` (Anton + Barlow), `oswald` (Oswald + Source Sans 3), `archivo` (Archivo Black + Archivo), `jakarta` (Plus Jakarta Sans), `manrope` (Manrope + Inter), `plex` (IBM Plex Sans + IBM Plex Serif), `plexmono` (IBM Plex Mono + IBM Plex Sans), `spacemono` (Space Mono + IBM Plex Sans), `firacode` (Fira Code + Fira Sans), `rubik` (Rubik + Karla), `nunito` (Nunito), `quicksand` (Quicksand + Nunito), `poppins` (Poppins + Inter), `montserrat` (Montserrat + Merriweather), `raleway` (Raleway + Lora), `josefin` (Josefin Sans + Lato), `syne` (Syne + Inter), `sora` (Sora + DM Sans), `lexend` (Lexend), `unbounded` (Unbounded + Figtree), `righteous` (Righteous + Nunito), `pacifico` (Pacifico + Quicksand), `caveat` (Caveat + Karla), `lobster` (Lobster + Lato), `arcade` (Press Start 2P + Space Mono), `terminal` (VT323 + IBM Plex Mono), `epilogue` (Epilogue + Inter), `youngserif` (Young Serif + Instrument Sans), `gloock` (Gloock + Hanken Grotesk), `bodoni` (Bodoni Moda + Figtree), `cinzel` (Cinzel + Lato), `comfortaa` (Comfortaa + Nunito), `fredoka` (Fredoka + Nunito), `atkinson` (Atkinson Hyperlegible), `literata` (Literata), `newsreader` (Newsreader + Inter), `schibsted` (Schibsted Grotesk + Source Serif 4), `shrikhand` (Shrikhand + Rubik), `monoton` (Monoton + Barlow), `kalam` (Kalam + Karla), `redhat` (Red Hat Display + Red Hat Text), `baloo` (Baloo 2 + Nunito), `librefranklin` (Libre Franklin + Libre Baskerville)\n\nPictures (op illus): `sparkle`, `leaf`, `sun`, `moon`, `food`, `fish`, `soup`, `cake`, `wave`, `art`, `mountain`, `bag`, `coffee`, `book`, `dumbbell`, `music`, `lantern`, `flame`, `target`, `lock`, `heart`, `star`, `plane`, `city`, `paw`, `house`, `car`, `pill`, `code`, `camera`, `ticket`, `globe`, `baby`, `chart`, `plant`, `gift`, `game`, `bolt`, `chat`, `users`, `play`, `check`, `note`\n\nIcons (op icon): `home`, `search`, `plus`, `user`, `users`, `settings`, `heart`, `chat`, `bell`, `cart`, `calendar`, `chart`, `check`, `play`, `pause`, `star`, `book`, `pin`, `clock`, `flame`, `leaf`, `dumbbell`, `wallet`, `music`, `image`, `back`, `chevron`, `more`, `close`, `lock`, `mail`, `gift`, `list`, `grid`, `sun`, `moon`, `bookmark`, `share`, `filter`, `camera`, `trophy`, `game`, `edit`, `trash`, `send`, `mic`, `globe`, `coffee`, `food`, `target`, `tag`, `bolt`, `sparkle`, `note`, `plane`, `house`, `ticket`, `soup`, `fish`, `mountain`, `car`, `wave`, `sliders`, `phone`, `cloud`\n\nTemplates (for `start`), by group:\n\n- Health: `habits`, `workout`, `meditation`, `water`, `sleep`, `meds`, `running`\n- Productivity: `notes`, `todo`, `planner`, `focus`, `kanban`, `journal`\n- Food: `recipes`, `noodles`, `coffee`, `mealprep`\n- Money: `budget`, `split`, `savings`, `subs`\n- Social: `chat`, `social`, `events`, `bookclub`, `neighbors`\n- Shopping: `shop`, `grocery`, `swap`, `drops`\n- Business: `booking`, `crm`, `invoices`, `stock`\n- Games: `gamemenu`, `puzzle`, `racing`, `party`\n- Travel: `trip`, `hotel`, `cityguide`\n- Learning: `flashcards`, `course`, `trivia`\n- Creator tools: `podcast`, `music`, `creator`\n- Family: `chores`, `baby`, `pets`\n- Utilities: `weather`, `plants`, `parking`, `converter`\n\n## 6. The steps (ops)\n\nEach step is `{ \"op\": …, … }`. `block` is a part id and `screen` a screen id from `app_read`.\n\n- `kit`: Add a ready-made screen or flow (settings, onboarding, profile …). The fastest way to a complete screen.\n  `{\"op\":\"kit\",\"kit\":\"settings\"}`\n- `screen.add`: Add an empty screen.\n  `{\"op\":\"screen.add\",\"name\":\"Menu\"}`\n- `screen.rename`: Rename a screen.\n  `{\"op\":\"screen.rename\",\"screen\":\"<screen id>\",\"name\":\"Orders\"}`\n- `screen.move`: Reorder a screen (place number from 0).\n  `{\"op\":\"screen.move\",\"screen\":\"<screen id>\",\"to\":1}`\n- `start`: Which screen the app opens on.\n  `{\"op\":\"start\",\"screen\":\"<screen id>\"}`\n- `block.add`: Add a part to a screen (at = place number; left out, it goes above the tab bar).\n  `{\"op\":\"block.add\",\"screen\":\"<screen id>\",\"type\":\"list\"}`\n- `block.move`: Move a part up (-1) or down (1).\n  `{\"op\":\"block.move\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"dir\":-1}`\n- `block.dup`: Copy a part.\n  `{\"op\":\"block.dup\",\"block\":\"<block id>\"}`\n- `block.remove`: Take a part out. Say why in the note.\n  `{\"op\":\"block.remove\",\"block\":\"<block id>\"}`\n- `text`: Change words. path is a key of the part that already holds words (title, sub, label, items.0.title).\n  `{\"op\":\"text\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"title\",\"value\":\"Good morning, Maya\"}`\n- `icon`: Change an icon (names: app_catalog).\n  `{\"op\":\"icon\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"items.0.icon\",\"value\":\"coffee\"}`\n- `illus`: Change a built-in picture (names: app_catalog).\n  `{\"op\":\"illus\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"illus\",\"value\":\"coffee\"}`\n- `item.add`: Entries of a list: item.add, item.dup, item.remove, item.up, item.down. path names the entry.\n  `{\"op\":\"item.add\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"items.0\"}`\n- `item.dup`: Copy an entry.\n  `{\"op\":\"item.dup\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"items.0\"}`\n- `item.remove`: Remove an entry.\n  `{\"op\":\"item.remove\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"items.1\"}`\n- `item.up`: Move an entry up.\n  `{\"op\":\"item.up\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"items.1\"}`\n- `item.down`: Move an entry down.\n  `{\"op\":\"item.down\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"items.0\"}`\n- `link`: A tap goes to a screen. path \"\" for the part itself (a button), items.2 for one entry. how: push, sheet, fade or morph.\n  `{\"op\":\"link\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"\",\"to\":\"<screen id>\",\"how\":\"push\"}`\n- `unlink`: Remove a link.\n  `{\"op\":\"unlink\",\"block\":\"<block id>\",\"path\":\"\"}`\n- `tabbar`: Add a tab bar across the main screens.\n  `{\"op\":\"tabbar\"}`\n- `preset`: A whole named look: colour, mode, type, corners, spacing, motion.\n  `{\"op\":\"preset\",\"id\":\"meadow\"}`\n- `theme`: Single controls of the look: accent, mode light|dark, radius sharp|soft|round|pill, font, density compact|regular|roomy, icons thin|regular|bold, motion calm|standard|snappy, surface neutral|warm|cool|tinted.\n  `{\"op\":\"theme\",\"set\":{\"mode\":\"dark\",\"accent\":\"#2F9E6E\"}}`\n- `rename`: The app's name.\n  `{\"op\":\"rename\",\"name\":\"Bean and Gone\"}`\n\n## 7. How to make it good\n\n### Start from the project\n\n- Read the project brief first. Say in one sentence what a person does with this app in their first minute. That is the first screen.\n- Three to six screens make a believable app: where you land, the thing you do, the detail, something about you, settings. Add ready-made flows with op kit before you build a screen by hand.\n- Every screen must be reachable from the first one, and every button must go somewhere (op link).\n\n### Screens that work\n\n| Kind of screen | Parts, in order | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Tracker (habits, fitness, money) | header → stats → checklist → chart → tabs | Today first, then history, then settings. |\n| Shop or menu (café, store, booking) | header → search → cards → list → button → tabs | Browse, look closer, order. |\n| Feed or community | nav → stories → feed → tabs | What is new, then a way to add to it. |\n| Tool (timer, calculator, notes) | nav → timer → segmented → button | One job on one screen, big controls. |\n| Settings screen | nav → settings → toggles → button | Grouped rows people already know. |\n\n### The craft\n\n**Layout**\n- One job per screen. A screen starts with a top bar or a big title, never both.\n- One main button per screen, at the bottom, where a thumb reaches. Other actions are quiet.\n- A tab bar has two to five tabs and only the screens people return to. Settings is a tab or sits behind the profile.\n- Lists show three to six entries in a design. Put the most telling ones first.\n\n**Type**\n- Pick one font for the app (op theme, font). Rounded and friendly for family, food and health; clean for tools and money; bold for games and events.\n- Titles are two to four words. Row labels are one line. Nothing a thumb must scroll sideways to read.\n\n**Spacing**\n- Choose density once (op theme, density): roomy for calm and few items, compact for data and lists.\n- Keep the same corners everywhere (radius). Mixed corners look unfinished.\n\n**Colour**\n- One accent, from the brand kit when there is one. It marks what can be tapped and what is on.\n- Dark mode is op theme { set: { mode: \"dark\" } }: one step changes every screen. Check the accent still reads; brighten it if not.\n- Start from a named look (op preset) that fits the mood, then set the accent.\n\n**Pictures**\n- You cannot upload pictures. Use the built-in pictures and icons (op illus, op icon) and name what the person should replace.\n- Icons in one row of a list come from the same family of meaning: all food, all actions.\n\n**Words**\n- Fill screens with data that fits this project: its own products, names and numbers from the brief, never \"Item 1\" or \"Lorem\".\n- Example people and amounts must look like examples (Maya, $4.50), not like real customers of the project.\n- Buttons say what happens: \"Add to order\", \"Start timer\".\n\n**Flows**\n- Lists and cards open a detail screen (how: push). Short tasks open a sheet (how: sheet). Tabs fade.\n- Settings needs a way in (a tab, or a row or icon on the profile) and a way back (a top bar).\n- Paywall, sign-in and checkout screens are drawings. Never claim a payment or an account works.\n\n**Access**\n- Tap targets are rows and buttons, not single words. Text on the accent must read (contrast 4.5 to 1).\n\n### What to avoid\n\n- A screen nothing leads to, or a button that goes nowhere. app_check lists both.\n- Ten parts on one screen. Split it.\n- A tab bar with one tab, or with more than five.\n- Placeholder data (\"Title\", \"Item\", \"Lorem ipsum\") and parts left exactly as they were added.\n- Changing the look screen by screen. The look is one setting for the whole app.\n\n## 8. Before you say it is done\n\n- [ ] app_check returns nothing you can fix: every screen is reachable, every button goes somewhere, no part is empty.\n- [ ] The first screen (start) is the one a new person should see.\n- [ ] Words and example data fit the project; nothing claims payments or sign-in work.\n- [ ] One accent, one font, one density; dark mode still reads if you switched it.\n- [ ] Your note says what you changed and why, then what the person should add or decide.\n\nThen tell the person, in plain words: what you proposed, where to approve it (Requests in App Creator, or their inbox), and what only they can do (pictures, the iPhone or Android look, working features, export).\n\n## 9. What you must never do\n\n- Export, publish or build the app. A person does that.\n- Remove screens (you cannot), switch between the iPhone and Android look, or add working features: a person does those in App Creator.\n- Edit the files in the project folder's app-web/ directory while TribeCode is connected: they are written from the design.\n- Work in a project you were not asked about, or copy anything from another project.\n- Invent facts about the project, real-looking customers or prices nobody gave you.\n- Approve your own proposal.\n\n## 10. A worked example: a settings screen and dark mode\n\n1. `project_brief`, then `app_read` with `detail: \"summary\"`: three screens, a tab bar on Home (`tab-1`) with Home and Orders, light mode, accent `#B4532C`.\n2. `app_propose` with `dry_run: true`:\n\n```json\n{ \"note\": \"Adds a Settings screen from the kit and switches the app to dark mode.\",\n  \"ops\": [ { \"op\": \"kit\", \"kit\": \"settings\" }, { \"op\": \"theme\", \"set\": { \"mode\": \"dark\" } } ] }\n```\n\n3. Send it (without `dry_run`). The answer lists the new screen (`settings`) and its parts with their ids. The kit made Settings a tab, so it is reachable. Its rows still say \"Jordan\" and \"jordan@example.com\": rewrite the ones this project would not have with `text` steps (`items.0.value`), and remove a group that does not apply with `block.remove`.\n4. `app_check` with `proposed: true`: every screen is reachable; the accent still reads on the dark background. If it did not: `{ \"op\": \"theme\", \"set\": { \"accent\": \"#E08A5B\" } }`.\n5. Tell the person: approve it under Requests; the Dark mode row is a drawing of a switch, and a working light and dark switch is a feature they add in App Creator.\n","MOTION":"# Making a motion graphic in TribeCode: the guide for AI agents\n\nYou are an AI working on a motion graphic in TribeCode's Motion Studio: a few seconds of animated interface, type or numbers for a launch post, a product page or a store listing. This file is everything you need. Follow it even if you have no other context.\n\n## 1. What you are working on\n\nA motion graphic is **one JSON document** saved in the project under a key (`launch-reel`). It is a list of **states** on a beat grid. One shape morphs from state to state (it is never cut); each state has a `kind` (button, slider, stat, word …), a place on the timeline (`at`, in beats), its words, and **acts** the cursor performs on it (click, drag, hover, type).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"v\": 1, \"name\": \"Launch reel\", \"aspect\": \"1:1\", \"bpm\": 120, \"bars\": 4, \"beatsPerBar\": 4, \"loop\": true,\n  \"themeName\": \"warm\", \"theme\": { \"bg\": \"#E9E6E1\", \"ink\": \"#111111\", \"paper\": \"#FFFFFF\", \"accent\": \"#B4532C\", \"muted\": \"#8A867F\" },\n  \"feel\": \"polished\", \"camera\": { \"strength\": 0.5, \"fill\": 0.66 }, \"cursor\": true, \"sounds\": true,\n  \"states\": [\n    { \"kind\": \"button\", \"at\": 0, \"text\": \"Order ahead\", \"acts\": [ { \"type\": \"click\", \"at\": 1, \"x\": 30, \"y\": 10 } ] },\n    { \"kind\": \"loader\", \"at\": 2 },\n    { \"kind\": \"check\", \"at\": 4, \"fill\": \"accent\" },\n    { \"kind\": \"stat\", \"at\": 6, \"value\": 365, \"label\": \"days a year, open from 7\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- Time is in beats: at 120 BPM a beat is half a second, `bars × beatsPerBar` beats in all.\n- Coordinates are units of a 1440 frame with the origin at the shape's centre. Per state: `w`, `h`, `r` (corner radius), `fill` (`ink`, `paper`, `accent`, `none` or a hex), `cx`, `cy`. Leave them out and the kind's own size is used.\n- Acts: `click { at, x, y }`, `drag { at, until, x0, y0, x1, y1 }`, `hover` (like drag, no press), `type { at, until }`, `focus { at }`.\n- Saving normalizes the document: unknown kinds are dropped and numbers are clamped, without an error. Read it back.\n\n## 2. The tools, in order\n\n| # | Call | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | `project_brief` | What this project is, its brand colours and voice. |\n| 2 | `motion_list` | The motion graphics in the project, and the templates, feels and kinds that exist. |\n| 3 | `motion_read` | One motion graphic as JSON. Read a template's result to learn a kind's words. |\n| 4 | `motion_write` | Saves a whole document under a key. `{ \"template\": \"<id>\", \"name\": …, \"accent\": \"#…\", \"aspect\": \"9:16\" }` starts from a template. |\n\nRules of the tools:\n\n- `motion_write` replaces the whole document. To change one, `motion_read` it, change what you mean to, and write all of it back.\n- A new piece gets a new key (lowercase words with dashes). Never write over a key you were not asked to change.\n- When the person's role needs an OK for motion graphics, your write becomes a change request and the answer says who approves. Nothing changes until they do.\n- There is no dry run and no check tool here: `motion_read` after writing is your check.\n\n## 3. What exists\n\nKinds of state:\n\n`button`, `loader`, `check`, `island`, `player`, `slider`, `toggle`, `tabs`, `chart`, `bars`, `donut`, `cmdk`, `toast`, `card`, `notif`, `logo`, `wordmark`, `word`, `list`, `stat`, `ring`, `phone`, `image`, `wipe`, `post`, `quote`, `poll`, `count`, `lower`, `caption`, `badge`, `scene`, `appicon`, `endcard`\n\nTemplates, by group:\n\n- ui: `ui-reel`, `ui-checkout`, `ui-player`, `ui-controls`, `ui-command`, `ui-notify`, `ui-onboard`, `ui-island`\n- promo: `app-preview`, `app-features`, `app-icon`, `app-whatsnew`, `app-carousel`, `app-download`\n- logo: `logo-draw`, `logo-stagger`, `logo-morph`, `logo-type`, `logo-icon`\n- type: `type-punch`, `type-quote`, `type-question`, `type-list`, `type-countdown`, `type-stack`\n- stat: `stat-big`, `stat-trio`, `stat-ring`, `stat-compare`\n- chart: `chart-line`, `chart-bars`, `chart-donut`, `chart-board`, `chart-story`\n- social: `social-post`, `social-hook`, `social-carousel`, `social-launch`, `social-quote`, `social-poll`, `social-milestone`, `social-live`, `soc-pov`, `soc-hot-take`, `soc-myth-fact`, `soc-three-words`, `soc-question-hook`, `soc-top-text`, `soc-one-liner`, `soc-stacked`, `soc-launch-916`, `soc-feature-drop`, `soc-release-notes`, `soc-teaser`, `soc-download`, `soc-beta-call`, `soc-coming-soon-916`, `soc-event-invite`, `soc-before-after-916`, `soc-day1-day30`, `soc-hours-built`, `soc-milestone-night`, `soc-weekly-recap`, `soc-highlight-4x5`, `soc-speedrun`, `soc-streak`, `soc-leaderboard`, `soc-split-compare`, `soc-new-look`, `soc-poll-916`, `soc-feedback-poll`, `soc-qa`, `soc-quote-4x5`, `soc-reply-card`, `soc-shoutout`, `soc-thank-you-4x5`, `soc-screen-spotlight`, `soc-click-to-edit`, `soc-search-reveal`, `soc-dark-mode`, `soc-tabs-tour`, `soc-notification`, `soc-island-music`, `soc-remix-this`, `soc-step-by-step-916`, `soc-link-in-bio`, `soc-live-916`, `soc-stream-169`, `soc-bts`, `soc-bug-fixed`, `soc-roadmap`, `soc-jam-recap`, `soc-tutorial-intro`, `soc-collab`, `soc-thread-opener`, `soc-countdown-169`, `soc-carousel-steps`, `soc-linkedin-update`, `soc-checkout-demo`, `soc-onboarding-916`, `soc-ask-flint`, `soc-stat-donut`, `soc-greeting`\n- tour: `tour-zoom`, `tour-wipe`, `tour-spot`, `tour-devices`\n- end: `end-remix`, `end-follow`, `end-cta`\n- trans: `trans-circle`, `trans-pill`, `trans-stack`\n- lower: `lower-name`, `lower-place`, `lower-live`\n- caption: `cap-karaoke`, `cap-pill`, `cap-outline`\n- share: `share-app-916`, `share-site-916`, `share-timelapse-916`, `share-app-11`, `share-site-11`, `share-timelapse-11`, `share-app-169`, `share-site-169`, `share-timelapse-169`\n\nFeels (how the springs, blur and camera behave):\n\n`crisp`, `snappy`, `calm`, `smooth`, `gentle`, `brisk`, `silky`, `steady`, `precise`, `lively`, `relaxed`, `firm`, `soft`, `swift`, `measured`, `breezy`, `tight`, `loose`, `polished`, `studio`, `launch`, `keynote`, `documentary`, `playful`, `elegant`, `minimal`, `bold`, `punchy`, `dreamy`, `cinematic`, `sharp`, `fluid`, `liquid`, `weighty`, `light`, `feather`, `glide`, `tactile`, `hushed`, `energetic`, `rapid`, `reveal`, `easygoing`, `confident`, `cleancut`, `velvet`, `metronome`, `heartbeat`, `paper`, `closeup`, `wide`, `social`, `demo`, `ambient`\n\nThemes: `warm`, `night`, `paper`, `ink`. Aspects: `1:1`, `9:16`, `16:9`, `4:5`.\n\n## 4. How to make it good\n\n- Read the project brief first. A motion graphic shows one idea in a few seconds: one product moment, one number, one line.\n- Start from a template (motion_write with { template }) and change its words and colours. Build states by hand only when no template fits.\n\n**Shape**\n- Four to eight states. Each state is on screen for at least two beats so it can be read.\n- One shape morphs through every state; it is never cut. Let neighbouring states differ in size or roundness so the morph shows.\n- Put states and acts on the beat (whole or half beats). Leave the last state a bar to rest before the loop.\n\n**Words**\n- Words in a state are three to five words. Real names and numbers from the brief; never placeholder text.\n\n**Colour**\n- The theme has five colours: bg, ink, paper, accent, muted. Use the brand kit's accent. Fill states with ink or paper and keep accent for the one state that matters.\n\n**Feel**\n- Pick one feel that fits the brand (calm, crisp, playful …) and keep the camera gentle (strength at most 0.6) unless the piece is a bold promo.\n- Loops must close: keep loop on and end where you began.\n\n### What to avoid\n\n- More than eight states, or states shorter than a beat.\n- A different colour on every state.\n- Cursor acts that click nothing: every click or drag lands on the shape of its own state.\n\n## 5. Before you say it is done\n\n- [ ] motion_read after writing: the state count, bars and tempo are what you meant (unknown kinds are dropped silently).\n- [ ] Every word is real and short; the accent is the brand's.\n- [ ] The piece loops cleanly and is under about 15 seconds.\n\nThen tell the person where it is (Motion Studio, by its name) and that exporting it (MP4, GIF, a section on the website) is theirs to do.\n\n## 6. What you must never do\n\n- Overwrite a motion graphic you were not asked to change: use a new key for a new piece.\n- Add music or pictures from addresses the person did not give.\n- Export or publish it. A person does that.\n"});
